Changes in the Windows fork (https://github.com/Azathothas/cadaver-windows).
Entries below this section are upstream's.

Changes in version 0.28-win3:
* `rget' and `rput': recursive download and upload, creating the
  collections and the local directories as they go.  `rput' reports a
  collection that is already there as such rather than as a failure,
  and skips the subtree under one it could not create, because nothing
  under a collection that is not there can succeed.  Either form given
  a plain resource does what `get' or `put' would have done.  Both
  refuse to go more than 64 levels deep, so a junction or a symbolic
  link that makes a directory contain itself is not walked forever.
  A MKCOL that comes back 405 means the URL is already mapped, which is
  not the same as a collection being there: `rput' asks what it is, and
  says so when it turns out to be a plain resource.
* Ctrl-C during a transfer abandons it and returns to the prompt.  It
  used to end the process, which during an upload leaves the server
  holding half a resource and no indication that anything went wrong.
  neon reads or writes a whole body inside one call and offers no
  cancel, so src/transfer.c is the same dispatch loops with the
  interrupt flag checked between blocks.  A second Ctrl-C during the
  same transfer ends the session as before, which is the way out of one
  that has stalled so completely that no block arrives to check the
  first.  `mput', `mget', `cat' and `less' with several arguments stop
  at that point rather than carrying on to the next file.
* The new `bench' command measures what this connection to this server
  can do: the round trip time of a PROPFIND, and the rate a PUT and a
  GET of a generated payload move at.  Rates are MiB/s and durations
  are wall clock.  --json carries a "benchmark" object; the rate in it
  is null where the duration reads as 0.000 seconds, because a rate
  worked out from a duration the document cannot express is one the
  numbers beside it do not support.
* Upstream #64 again, in two more commands.  `label' concatenated the
  label into the request body and `search' did the same with the value
  in a comparison, so `label res add "a&b"' and `search displayname =
  "a&b"' both produced a body that was not well-formed and a 400 from
  the server.  Both are escaped now, the way `set lockowner' has been
  since 0.28-win2.  A property name in a search query becomes an element
  name and cannot be escaped, so one that is not a name is refused --
  including one that came from `set searchorder'.  The scope of a search
  is a URI path, and an ampersand is a sub-delimiter that survives
  ne_path_escape(), so a search in a collection called "a&b" produced
  one too.  None of this was reachable before there was a server to
  answer SEARCH and LABEL.
* `rget' refuses a member name the server chose that is not one path
  segment.  It is the only command that writes a local file under a name
  the server picked rather than one the user typed, and it joined that
  name to the destination unchecked: a member href of
  "..%2F..%2Fescaped.txt" unescapes to "../../escaped.txt", and against
  a server that returns one, `rget' wrote outside the directory it was
  given.  A name that is empty, "." or "..", or that holds a path
  separator -- or a colon on Windows, where one names an alternate data
  stream -- is now reported and skipped.
* A path outside the active ANSI code page works.  cadaver re-decodes
  its command line from the wide form and hands UTF-8 around, and
  lib/system.c then opens, stats and renames with the C runtime's
  narrow calls, which read the active code page: `lcd' into a directory
  called 日本語 reported "No such file or directory", and `put' could
  not open a file that was sitting there.  The executable now carries
  an application manifest making UTF-8 the process code page, which
  Windows 10 version 1903 and later honour.
* The DeltaV commands no longer put a trailing slash on the path of a
  plain resource.  `version', `checkin', `checkout', `uncheckout',
  `history' and `label' resolved with uri_resolve_native_coll(), which
  adds one unconditionally, and DeltaV is nearly always applied to a
  plain resource, where /x/ and /x are two different things.  Nothing
  had ever caught it: no test server answered any of those methods.
* What one command has been told about a resource is remembered for the
  length of that command.  A command asks what a path is before doing
  anything with it, and expanding a remote wildcard asked twice more
  per match -- once through the glob's stat callback and once to
  resolve the expanded name -- although the listing that produced the
  match had already said.  Against the Go test server, `delete *.txt'
  over twenty files went from twenty-two PROPFINDs to three.
* `set lsformat' says how one line of an `ls' listing is laid out: the
  name, the full path, the size in bytes or rounded in powers of 1024,
  the modification time in local form or as ISO 8601 UTC, the type as a
  marker or as a word, the executable flag and the version-control
  state.  `describe lsformat' prints the list and `unset lsformat'
  returns to the layout `ls' has always had.
* `lls' takes -a, and without it leaves out a name beginning with a
  dot.  That is what a wildcard already did: `mput *' never matched
  one, so the listing was showing what an upload would skip.
* The progress indicator writes a byte count instead of dots when the
  output is not a terminal.  One dot per transfer callback left a line
  of hundreds of them in a log and no number at all.  A finished
  transfer reports "[28 bytes]" beside "succeeded."; a long one says
  how far it has got every ten seconds.  Nothing is reported beside a
  failure, where what arrived was the error body.
* `set' takes on and off for a boolean option.  `set' with no argument
  displays every one of them as on or off, and until now that could not
  be typed back in: a boolean refused a value outright, so `unset' was
  the only way to turn one off.
* `set searchdepth' reports a value it does not know instead of
  treating it as infinity, so a typo no longer asks for the widest
  possible search in silence.  `unset' on an option that does not exist
  is a failed command, as `set' on one already was.
* An empty argument can be written.  gettoken() returned NULL for a
  zero-length token and parse_command() reads NULL as the end of the
  line, so `propset res name ""' set nothing and dropped every argument
  after it without a word.
* An option name completes after `set', `unset' and `describe'.  The
  command table has said so since the completion scope became
  per-argument; the completion function had that case falling through
  to no matches.
* `copy', `move' and `rename' onto a resource that is already there say
  which resource it was.  The request carries Overwrite: F and the
  server answers 412, which names neither the resource in the way nor
  the option that would replace it.  `rename' sends Overwrite: F
  whatever the option says, so it reports that `move' is the command
  that can overwrite.
* --json carries what was printed outside any command -- the connection
  banner, the closing message.  It was captured into a buffer that was
  never written, although AGENTS.md has always documented the run's
  "output" field.
* `-r' naming a file that cannot be read is a usage error, reported
  before anything connects.  It used to print a message, run nothing at
  all and exit 0: the failure belonged to no command, and the exit
  status counts commands.
* Every request in a --trace is tagged with the command that caused it.
  The label was set after the line had been parsed, so the requests a
  wildcard expansion makes were tagged "no command", which is when the
  tag is most wanted.  A wildcard now appears twice, as the pattern and
  as what it matched.
* `search' reports its outcome where every other command does, and
  prints the results after it.  It opened no operation at all, so the
  SEARCH it made was recorded against nothing; a search that matched
  nothing printed nothing; and the media type in a result line was cut
  to ten characters, which made "application/octet-stream" into
  "applicatio".
* `history' closes the operation it opens.  Nothing did on success, so
  the record carried a duration measured from the start of the command
  and no outcome.  `label' with an action that is not add, set or
  remove reports it the way every other failure is reported.
* Bug fix: getrestype() dereferenced a null pointer when a PROPFIND
  came back as a multistatus that named no resource at all.
* Bug fix: configure.ac never called AC_CANONICAL_HOST, so $host_os was
  empty and the Windows branch of the platform check never ran.  The
  autotools build on Windows reported "posix" and added no -lws2_32 of
  its own; it linked anyway, because neon's macros bring that library
  in, which is why nothing noticed.
* Bug fix: run_search() left the request, the XML parser, the body
  buffer and both property lists behind on every search that did not
  work, and execute_chexec() leaked the URL when its first argument was
  not + or -.
* Bug fix: `cat a b' and `less a b' did nothing at all after an
  interrupted transfer.  Both read the interrupt flag as their loop
  condition, and nothing cleared it between commands.
* Bug fix: `bench' with a ten-digit size and a G suffix multiplied it
  into a signed 64-bit overflow, which is undefined rather than large.
  The size and the count are both parsed with their bounds checked
  before each step, and the payload is allocated with malloc() so that a
  gigabyte the machine cannot provide is reported rather than aborting
  the process.
* Bug fix: the resource cache keyed on ne_path_compare(), which ignores
  case.  That is right where neon uses it and wrong as a cache key,
  because /Foo and /foo are two resources on a case-sensitive server.
* Tests:
 - tests/godav/deltav.go answers SEARCH and the DeltaV methods, which
   x/net/webdav does not implement and wsgidav does not either.  The
   commands that use them had been compiled, linked and never run.
   That was the largest untested surface in the tree, and the first
   run against it found the trailing slash above.
 - The Go test server can pace the response and request bodies under
   one prefix, so that a transfer lasts long enough to be interrupted
   or measured; everything here is a local disk over the loopback
   interface.
 - tests/godav.sh checks that each special prefix covers the name of
   every session that wants that subtree and of no other.  The
   collection a session runs in is named after the session, so the
   coupling was real and was a comment.
 - Five more scripted sessions: recursive transfer, bench, listings,
   COPY and MOVE onto an existing resource, and DASL with DeltaV.
   tests/bench_check.py looks at the numbers bench reported, which
   tests/normalise.py has to replace in the transcript.
 - tests/sessions/glob.check counts the requests one command made, so
   that losing the resource cache has something to fail.
 - The Go test server can name a member outside the collection in every
   PROPFIND under one prefix, and the `escape' session checks that
   `rget' writes nothing outside the directory it was given.
 - tests/offline.sh is 61 checks rather than 43, and looks for two more
   ways to write to standard output: a stdio call naming stdout, and a
   write to the descriptor.
* Verified by hand, because the harness cannot: Ctrl-C during a paced
  4194304-byte (4.0 MiB) transfer, tab completion of an option name in
  a real Windows console, and a directory called 日本語 holding
  テスト.txt.

Changes in version 0.28-win2:
* The exit status says what happened.  0 if every command succeeded,
  otherwise the number that failed, capped at 125 so that it cannot be
  read as a shell's signalled exit.  A command line that could not be
  understood at all exits 2, having run nothing.  Upstream exits 0
  however the session went, which is why AGENTS.md used to say that
  parsing the transcript was the only option.
 - `--help' and `--version' exit 0 rather than -1, which Windows
   reports as 4294967295 and a shell with `set -e' in it treats as a
   failure of something that in fact worked.
* --json writes one JSON object to standard output and nothing else:
  every command with its arguments, the request target, whether it
  worked, how long it took in wall-clock seconds, the reason and the
  HTTP method and status when it did not, and everything it printed.
  `ls', `propget', `propnames', `head', the lock commands, `pwd' and
  `set' also report what they found as structured fields rather than
  only as text.  The shape is documented as a contract in AGENTS.md.
 - `cat', `less' and `edit' are refused under --json: each writes a
   byte stream to standard output or runs a program that might, and
   standard output carries the result document.
 - Everything cadaver prints now goes through src/output.c, which is
   what makes that guarantee keepable.  tests/offline.sh greps the
   source for a write to standard output that does not.
* --trace[=FILE] dumps every request and response, tagged with the
  command that issued it, so a failing session is diagnosable without
  a proxy.  Message bodies are included, except the body of a
  transfer, which is the resource itself.  --verbose widens it to
  everything neon reports except the credentials in the clear.
* Upstream #75: a quote character in a .netrc value is no longer
  removed.  The tokeniser treated a quote as opening a quoted run
  wherever it met one, so a generated password with an apostrophe in
  the middle of it silently lost that character and every request
  failed to authenticate with nothing to say why.  A quote now quotes
  only when it opens a value, which is the convention the format has
  and still the way to write a value containing a space.
* Upstream #64: the lock owner is XML-escaped.  It goes into the LOCK
  request body, which neon concatenates verbatim, so `set lockowner
  "foo&bar"' produced a body that was not well-formed and a 400 from
  the server.
* Upstream #54: `mv collection newname' renames the collection again.
  A destination that does not exist yet is a rename; only one that
  exists and is not a collection is refused, which is what the check
  was meant to say.
* Upstream #25: a .netrc entry that supplies a login and no password
  is used for the login, and only the password is asked for.  It used
  to be ignored entirely, so both were prompted for and the file
  looked broken.
* Upstream #4: `cd' into a collection the server did not report as one
  says so, and points at -t, rather than asking whether the location
  is WebDAV-enabled.  A server that answers the PROPFIND perfectly
  well but writes <collection/> without the DAV namespace lands here,
  and the old message sent the reporter looking in the wrong place.
* A failed `get' leaves the local file as it found it.  The download
  goes to a temporary name beside the destination and is renamed over
  it once the whole body has arrived; the old code created the
  destination before making the request, so one 404 left a zero-length
  file that made every retry prompt for a name instead of downloading.
* The new `clobber' option, and --clobber, say what `get' does when the
  local file exists: ask, which is the default and what it always did,
  yes to overwrite, or no to refuse and fail.  Asking cannot work in a
  script, where the prompt reads end of input.
* `resumeget' puts the local file back to the length it had when the
  request fails, so that a server which ignores the Range header and
  answers 200 with the whole resource cannot leave it longer than it
  was.  TODO said neon appended a second copy in that case; it does not
  -- dispatch_to_fd() writes to the descriptor only for a 206 -- and the
  new `norange' session, which arranges a server that ignores Range,
  is what established that.  The guarantee is kept here anyway rather
  than by inspection of a vendored library.
* `put' and `mput' report a directory as one.  Opening a directory
  gives EACCES on Windows, so `mput *' used to report "Could not open
  file: Permission denied" for each, which was true of neither.
* `put' names its destination the way every other command does.  It
  passed the escaped URI path straight to the output, so uploading
  "has space.txt" reported /dav/has%20space.txt where deleting the
  same resource reported /dav/has space.txt.
* `unlock' on a resource with no stored lock says so.  With the input
  at end of file the prompt for a lock token read nothing and the
  command ended in silence: no message, no failure line, nothing to
  tell it from success.
* `head' reports a failure.  It printed the status and headers when it
  got them and nothing at all when it did not, so a HEAD that failed
  left no trace in the transcript.  It now announces itself and
  reports the outcome the way `ls' does.
* `discover' and `steal' distinguish a server that reported no locks
  from one that could not answer.  Both used to read "no locks found".
* Tab completion, verified in a real Windows console for the first
  time, now works for the commands that take one remote argument and
  one of something else.  `get', `put', `resumeget', `propget',
  `propdel', `propset', `chexec' and `label' offered nothing at all,
  because the completion scope was a property of the command rather
  than of the argument.
* Bug fix: search_netrc() stopped at the first `default' entry, so a
  default written above a named one shadowed it and cadaver
  authenticated as somebody else with no indication that the entry it
  was asked to use had been passed over.  The host comparison also
  ignores case now, as host names do.
* Bug fix: execute_command() freed one fewer token than it allocated,
  leaking the last argument of every command.
* From upstream pull request #74: `propnames' marks each name with the
  bullet that `propget' and `head' use.
* Tests:
 - tests/netrc.sh, 26 unit tests for lib/netrc.c.  What that parser
   gets wrong is invisible in use: a password it mangles produces an
   authentication failure that names neither the file nor the
   character.
 - Nine more scripted sessions: the exit status, --json, --trace,
   `clobber', renaming a collection, a lock owner containing markup,
   a server that ignores Range, and two that authenticate from a .netrc
   against a subtree of the Go test server that now requires it -- one
   with a password holding an apostrophe, one supplying only the login.
 - The Go test server can be told to require authentication under one
   prefix and to ignore Range under another, because no real server
   here does either and both have to be exercised.
 - Every session pins its exit status on the last line of the
   transcript.
 - A session may bring a .flags file of extra command-line arguments,
   a .home holding a .netrc, a .stdin, and a .servers naming the
   servers it applies to.
 - tests/offline.sh is 43 checks rather than 18.

Changes in version 0.28-win1:
* cadaver builds and runs on Windows as a native MinGW-w64 program: no
  Cygwin runtime, no WSL, no MSYS2 needed at run time. A release is one
  statically linked cadaver.exe that depends only on DLLs that ship
  with Windows.
* Binary transfers are no longer corrupted. Every open() and fopen()
  cadaver makes for a transfer now asks for binary mode on any platform
  that translates line endings, not only on OS/2 and Cygwin. Without
  this every `put', `get', `edit' and `cat' of anything that is not
  plain text gained a carriage return before each newline on Windows.
* The character encoding of the terminal is worked out from the console
  code page rather than from nl_langinfo(), which Windows does not
  have. The console is switched to UTF-8 for the lifetime of the
  process and put back at exit, so names outside ASCII survive both
  ways. The fallback that would have been compiled on a platform
  without nl_langinfo() never compiled at all: it had a stray `||' in
  it, which is why nobody had built cadaver on such a platform.
* The command line is re-decoded from the wide form on Windows, so an
  argument that the active ANSI code page cannot represent still
  reaches cadaver intact.
* Paths:
 - The home directory comes from $HOME, then the Windows profile
   directory, so ~/.cadaverrc, ~/.netrc and ~/.cadaver-locks are found
   without $HOME being set by hand.
 - A backslash in a command is an ordinary character on Windows,
   because it is the path separator, so `lcd C:\Users\me' and
   `lls C:\dir\*' both work.  Quotes are the way to write a name
   containing a space or a hash there; elsewhere a backslash still
   quotes the character after it, so `get my\ file' is unchanged.
 - base_name() understands both separators and a drive prefix, so
   `put C:\dir\file.txt' uploads to `file.txt' rather than to a name
   containing the whole path.
 - `edit' puts its temporary file in the directory Windows nominates
   rather than in /tmp, and quotes the file name when it runs the
   editor, which the Windows temporary directory routinely needs.
* Commands that needed a Unix to work:
 - `lls' lists the directory itself, in the layout the remote `ls'
   uses, rather than forking an `ls' that Windows has neither the call
   nor the program for. It takes an optional directory and -l.
 - `less' opens the pager with _popen() in binary mode; the default is
   $PAGER, then more, which ships with Windows.
 - `edit' runs $VISUAL, then $EDITOR, then notepad.
 - `cat' puts its own standard output into binary mode for the length
   of the transfer, so a resource arrives on the far end of a pipe
   byte for byte.
 - The password prompt turns echo off through the console handle
   rather than through termios, and restores it on the way out and
   from the SIGINT handler.
* Filename globbing is a new implementation, lib/glob.c, replacing the
  copy of glibc's glob from 1998. It supports *, ?, [set], [a-z],
  [!set] and POSIX character classes, honours GLOB_ALTDIRFUNC so that
  remote expansion is unchanged, treats a backslash as a separator
  rather than a quote when matching the local file system on Windows,
  and matches case-insensitively there. Results are sorted. A literal
  path segment is joined on without reading a directory, so a pattern
  costs one PROPFIND per collection it actually has to expand.
 - Interrupting a slow remote expansion with Ctrl-C now works. The old
   code set a flag that the bundled glob only read under #ifdef SHELL,
   which was never defined.
* One executable and one version. VERSION at the top of the tree is
  read by configure and by Makefile.w32, so the two build paths cannot
  report different numbers.
* Tests, where there were none:
 - tests/offline.sh: 18 checks that need no server, no network and no
   Python.
 - tests/glob.sh: 48 unit tests for lib/glob.c, against a synthetic
   directory tree and a real one.
 - tests/godav.sh and tests/wsgidav.sh: ten scripted sessions run
   against golang.org/x/net/webdav and against wsgidav, with the
   transcripts compared against checked-in expected output. Neither
   server is complete on its own: x/net/webdav locks correctly but has
   no dead property store, wsgidav has dead properties but answers
   LOCK with a Content-Type that is not a media type. Both gates run
   in CI.
* Bug fix: `help' ended its command list with a carriage return when
  the last row was short, which ran the aliases line into the last
  command on a terminal and left a stray CR in a redirected
  transcript.
* Bug fix: `edit' judged whether the file changed by its timestamp
  alone, so an edit made and saved within the same second was reported
  as no change and never uploaded. The size is compared as well.
* Bug fix: cad_mkstemp() required the XXXXXX at the very end of the
  template, but `edit' appends the resource's extension after it, so
  every `edit' of a resource with an extension failed to create its
  temporary file.
* Bug fix: the remote directory reader leaked the converted name of
  every entry it returned, so a tab-completion or a remote glob leaked
  once per entry.
* Bug fix: a file size over 2 GiB is read with a 64-bit stat, so `lls'
  reports it correctly and `resumeget' resumes from the right offset
  rather than from a truncated one.
* Build: the bundled getopt, snprintf, strcasecmp, strftime, fnmatch,
  tempname and expat copies are gone, along with the unused dirname.
  mingw-w64 and every libc cadaver has ever built against provide the
  first four; the glob rewrite removed the need for fnmatch; nothing
  called dir_name(); lib/expat was a copy of expat from 1999 that no
  build rule referenced.
* Build: NLS is not built. The translations needed gettext at build
  time and a locale directory at run time, which a single self-
  contained executable cannot carry. The _() macros are still in the
  source and still resolve to the identity, so the strings are ready
  if it is ever wanted back.
* Documentation: README.md, AGENTS.md, PATCHES.md, TODO, INSTALL, FAQ
  and the manual page were all rewritten for this fork.  INSTALL was
  the generic autoconf text, which sent at least one reporter looking
  for a configure script inside src/ (upstream issue 72); it now says
  what to install and which two commands to run.  TODO records every
  open upstream issue with an analysis of what is actually wrong.
* neon 0.37.1 is bundled as ordinary files rather than as a submodule,
  with four portability patches. See PATCHES.md.

Changes in release 0.28:
* Add 'resumeget', 'head' commands.
* Cosmetic tweak for 'propget' command.
* Allow using 'edit' on an already-locked resource.
* 'edit' uses a conditional PUT (where possible) to avoid conflicts.

Changes in release 0.27:
* Opening a location which does not indicate DAV class 1 support
  is now an error (or a warning in tolerant mode).
* Removed obsolete BUGS, INTEROP, FAQ documents.
* Fixed memory leaks in 'cat', 'less' commands.
* Rewrite of remote tab-completion to work for arbitrary paths.
* Fixes and improvements for glob (filename expansion) support:
 - glibc glob() will be used if available in preference to bundled code.
 - remote wildcards are now expanded across paths (e.g. 'rm */*.html').
* Add client-cert-uri option for neon 0.35+.
* Update bundled neon to 0.35.0; neon 0.29.x or later required.

Changes in release 0.26:
* Argument passed to "cadaver" must now be an absolute URI,
  various pseudo-URLs are now treated as errors.
* Rewrite of path and character set handling, fixing support
  for locales using non-UTF-8 character encoding:
 - URI paths are translated to/from the native character encoding
 - nl_langinfo() used to detect UTF-8 locale support
 - full relative path resolution is now done for paths
 - "get" now uses a locale-native default filename (issue #33)
 - iconv (or libiconv) is required for non-UTF-8 native locales
* Fix memory leak in remote filename completion.
* Various commands will now automatically act on the destination
  of a redirect as required rather than failing.
* Rewrite of 'copy' and 'move' commands:
 - a source collection is moved/copied inside a destination coll
   rather than overwriting it.
 - more error cases are disallowed (e.g. 'mv / /foo')
 - a source collection with a non-collection destination now fails
* New 'rename' command added for MOVE with 'Overwrite: F'
 - this ignores the 'overwrite' boolean option unlike cp/mv
* Various minor command fixes:
 - propget: output improved for clarity.
 - rmcol: lock handling on error path fixed.
 - less: catch errors from pager command.
* Added 'systemproxy' option to enable libproxy.
* Update to neon 0.34.0.

Changes in release 0.25:
* Support bundled neon build again.

Changes in release 0.24:
* Modernize configure scripts (Hugh McMaster)
 - fixing cross-compilation support
* Various spelling fixes (Hugh McMaster)
* Cosmetic fix for "cat" output
* Bundled neon support is not supported
* Support building against neon up to 0.32

Changes in release 0.23.3:
* Update to neon 0.29.1.

Changes in release 0.23.2:
* Fix "make install" with NLS builds.

Changes in release 0.23.1:
* Update to neon 0.28.0.

Changes in release 0.23.0:
* Update to neon 0.27.0; drop support for older versions of neon.
* Fix "search contains" (thanks to Jack Bates)
* Add DESTDIR support to "make install" (Dr Volker Zell)

Changes in release 0.22.5:
